[005] — WhyChoose polish pass (dish, icons, typography, pointer field)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/WhyChoose.tsx`, `app/globals.css`.
A refinement of [004], not a rebuild. The assembly structure, scatter geometry,
stagger timing and mobile fallback from [004] are all unchanged.

**Change — the dish**
1. Strokes went from flat `#005DAC` @ 1.5 / opacity .55 to a
   `url(#wcDishGrad)` gradient (`#7FCBFF → #0096FA → #005DAC`) @ 2.25 /
   opacity .85, so the outline reads as a lit object rather than a wireframe.
2. Two **overlay** ellipses (`data-rim="mid"` / `data-rim="inner"`) sit on top
   of the drawn mid/inner rims and carry a travelling dash (52s one way, 41s
   the other). *Rotating the real ellipses was tried and rejected* — with
   `rx ≠ ry` a rotation visibly tumbles the ring instead of spinning it.
   Travelling dashes read as rotation and stay flat. They are separate elements
   because a repeating `stroke-dasharray` would otherwise clobber the
   `dasharray: 1` the scroll draw-in depends on.
3. `[data-dish-glow]` — a blurred radial behind the reflector centre, breathing
   scale/blur on a 4.2s sine loop.
4. The whole dish is wrapped in a `motion.div` carrying
   `transformPerspective: 1100` + `rotateX/rotateY` (±7–8°) + drift, spring-
   lagged (`stiffness 55, damping 20`). Perspective is set via
   `transformPerspective`, not a `perspective` class — the latter only affects
   children, not the element's own rotation.

**Change — icons**
Flat white tile → glass chip (`bg-white/50`, `backdrop-blur-md`, inset
highlight, `rounded-2xl`). Hover springs to 1.09 and the glow blurs wider;
`icon-stroke-redraw` (globals.css) re-draws the lucide strokes on hover. Each
icon floats on its own phase (2.4 + i·0.37s) so the row never lifts as a slab.

**Change — typography / connectors**
Numbers are now `tracking-[0.28em]` in `brand-blue-bright/90`. Each title has a
gradient underline (`wcUnderGrad`) that draws in at `at + 0.26`, just after its
icon seats. Connectors became SVG: `data-wc-link` draws in solid, and a
separate `data-wc-flow` line scales open behind it carrying dashes that travel
*toward* the dish (`data-flow="out"|"in"` per side).

**Change — interaction**
A single normalised pointer field on the `<section>` (`ptrX`/`ptrY`, −0.5…0.5)
feeds every spring, so the canvas responds as one field rather than via
per-element listeners. Guarded by `prefers-reduced-motion`.

**Critical invariant honoured:** GSAP owns `[data-wc-icon]`'s transform (scatter
flight + idle float); Framer's hover spring was moved to a **nested inner
span**. The two libraries never write the same element's transform.

**Verified**
`npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · `npx next build` succeeded, 12/12 static pages ·
dev server compiles clean (the `Unexpected token 'section'` errors in the
console buffer are **stale** — they quote a single-line `<section>` that no
longer exists; `grep` for it exits 1) · DOM confirms 5 pairs, 5 icons, 4
connectors + 4 flow lines (pair 05 is `side: bottom`, correctly has none), 5
underlines, both rim overlays, the glow, and all three gradients resolving
(`stroke="url(#wcDishGrad)"`, width 2.25) · zero elements stranded at
`opacity: 0` / `visibility: hidden` / zero-matrix · end-state layout measured
with the start-states cleared: dish centred at x 435–835, pairs 01/03 left,
02/04 right, 05 centred beneath, glow concentric with the reflector, no
horizontal overflow.

**Not verified — flagged honestly:** the preview pane reports
`document.hidden: true` and **0 rAF frames**, and GSAP's ticker is rAF-driven,
so no scrub or idle loop can advance in it. Every animated value stays frozen
at its `.from()` start. Structure, build, console and assembled layout are
confirmed; the *motion itself* — tilt feel, dash travel speed, glow breathe —
has not been watched and needs an eye on a real browser.

**Revert**
Restore `components/WhyChoose.tsx` to the [004] satellite-dish implementation
(flat `#005DAC` strokes @ 1.5, plain white icon tiles, `<span>` connectors with
`scaleX`, no pointer field, no `ReasonPair` component — the markup was inline in
the `REASONS.map()`), and delete the `icon-stroke-redraw` keyframes plus the
`[data-wc-pair]:hover [data-wc-icon] svg > *` rule from `app/globals.css`.
[004]'s structure is intact underneath this pass, so reverting is safe.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[006] — WhyChoose fixes: dish geometry, azimuth sweep, icon float containment
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/WhyChoose.tsx`, `app/globals.css`.

**Fix 1 — the dish read as a blob/eye, not a satellite dish**
`DISH_PATHS` replaced. Was three *concentric full ellipses* (which is what made
it read as an eye) on a floating chevron. Now a prime-focus dish:
- `rim` — the reflector opening (one ellipse, not three).
- `bowlOuter` / `bowlInner` — interior contours that arc **upward** across the
  face (`A120 44 0 0 0`). This is what makes the reflector read as a concave
  bowl; concentric rings read as flat.
- `strutLeft/Right/Centre` + `hornCone` + a horn circle — feed at the focal point.
- `neck`, `mast`, `legLeft/Right/Front`, `footLeft/Right/Front` — a real tripod.
  The base had been reduced to a chevron; it is now a mast with three splayed
  legs and feet.
Removed: `rimOuter/rimMid/rimInner`, `legs`, `pad`, `data-dish-scan`.

**Fix 2 — dish now sweeps 360°, lighting each card in turn**
Removed the alternating dash-travel ring idle from [005] entirely.
A single proxy tween drives `azimuth.deg` 0→360 over `SWEEP_DURATION` (26s),
`repeat: -1`, `ease: 'none'`. Its `onUpdate`:
1. Sets `[data-dish-spin]` `scaleX = cos θ` (floored at 0.08 so it never fully
   vanishes edge-on) plus a ±5° `rotate` lean. **A flat 2D `rotate` was wrong** —
   it spins the dish face like a pinwheel. Real azimuth rotation foreshortens the
   ellipse, and `scaleX` going negative past 90° is correct: that is the moment
   we begin seeing the dish's back.
2. Splits the sweep into 5 × 72° segments and stamps `data-lit="true"` on the
   matching pair, in clockwise visual order `SWEEP_ORDER = ['01','02','04','05','03']`.
Only the reflector group spins; the tripod is outside `[data-dish-spin]` and
stays put, as a real mount does.

**Fix 3 — icons floated far enough to overlap text**
Root cause: the idle float wrote `y` on `[data-wc-icon]`, the *same element* the
scroll assembly's `.from()` writes `x/y/rotate/scale` on. The two tweens fought
over one transform, and the resulting values were what let icons wander into the
headline and body copy. The float now drives a new dedicated `[data-wc-bob]`
element nested inside `[data-wc-icon]`, amplitude cut from 4px to **3px** and
period slowed (3.2s + i·0.4). Three nested nodes now, one owner each:
`[data-wc-icon]` GSAP assembly → `[data-wc-bob]` GSAP float → `motion.span`
Framer hover.

**Also fixed — the [005] hover effect was never running**
The radar highlight was first written with Tailwind `group-data-[lit=true]:`
arbitrary variants. Tailwind's JIT **never emitted those rules** — verified 0
matching rules in the served CSS. Rewritten as plain CSS in `globals.css`
(`[data-wc-pair][data-lit='true'] …`). Worth noting the same class of bug means
the `[data-wc-pair]:hover [data-wc-icon]` stroke-redraw added in [005] is present
in the served CSS and does resolve — that one was authored as plain CSS and is fine.

**Verified**
`npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · dev server compiles, `GET / 200`, **no server errors** ·
0 orphan references to the removed `rimOuter/rimMid/rimInner/data-rim/data-dish-scan/legs/pad` ·
16 `[data-dish-draw]` strokes present; `[data-dish-spin]` bbox y 30–296 (reflector
+ horn + neck) inside a full svg bbox y 30–420, so the tripod renders below it and
is outside the spinning group · sweep math replayed across a full 360°: exactly 5
transitions at 0/72/144/216/288°, all five cards, correct order · `scaleX`/lean at
quadrants = 1/0, 0.08/+5, −1/0, −0.08/−5 as intended · highlight verified on all
5 pairs with transitions disabled: chip `#005DAC → #0096FA`, connector
`2px → 3.5px`, title `#0E4275 → #005DAC` (pair 05 has no connector by design) ·
`[data-wc-bob]` measures exactly the icon slot (44×44) and, expanded by the full
±3px float, **no icon box intersects its text box** on any of the five (gaps
28–168px).

**Not verified — flagged honestly:** the preview pane still reports 0 rAF frames,
so GSAP's ticker never advances there. The sweep tween, the icon float and all
CSS transitions stay frozen at their start values in that pane — which is why the
highlight only measured as working once transitions were force-disabled. The
*logic* is confirmed by replay and by direct state toggling; the sweep actually
turning, at the speed it turns, needs an eye on a real browser.

**Revert**
Restore `components/WhyChoose.tsx` to the [005] implementation (concentric
`rimOuter/rimMid/rimInner` ellipses, `data-rim` dash-travel idle, `data-dish-scan`,
`legs`/`pad` base, float on `[data-wc-icon]`, no `[data-wc-bob]`, no
`[data-dish-spin]`, no `SWEEP_*` tunables) and delete the
"WhyChoose: radar-sweep highlight" block from `app/globals.css`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[007] — WhyChoose: hand-drawn dish replaced with a sourced CC0 illustration
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/WhyChoose.tsx`, plus new `public/vendor/`.

**Why** — every previous dish in this section ([004]–[006]) was hand-authored from
SVG primitives and none of them read as a satellite dish. Per instruction, the
geometry is now a sourced asset and no longer drawn by us.

**Asset**
"Axial reflecting dish with monopod antenna and beams Pinhead icon", Wikimedia
Commons, **CC0 1.0** — verified by reading `imageinfo → extmetadata →
LicenseShortName` from the MediaWiki API, not from a search snippet. Original file
and licence record committed at `public/vendor/satellite-dish-cc0.svg` and
`.LICENSE.txt`. A single filled path in a 0 0 15 15 viewBox: dish, monopod feed,
pedestal base with splayed foot, two beam arcs.

Two SVG Repo candidates (#216854, #204611) were downloaded, recoloured and
previewed, then **ruled out** — their licence could not be verified (the pages are
client-rendered and carry no licence text in the HTML), which is not worth the
risk on a commercial site.

**Change**
1. `DISH_PATHS` (15 hand-authored primitives) deleted; replaced by the `DISH_ART`
   path constant + `DISH_CENTRE`. Path data is byte-for-byte as sourced —
   asserted equal against the vendored file, 603 chars both sides. Only the fill
   is ours (`url(#wcDishGrad)`).
2. Dish `<svg>` viewBox 0 0 400 430 → **0 0 15 15** (the asset's own box); glow
   ring recentred to 7.5/7.5 r 6.6, stroke 0.12.
3. **The stroke draw-in had to go.** The old reveal ran `strokeDashoffset` across
   16 stroked elements; the sourced artwork is one *filled* path with no stroke,
   so a dashoffset reveal has nothing to act on. Replaced with a
   `scale 0.62 → 1` + `rotate -28 → 0` settle on `[data-dish-art]`. Still
   transform-only, so the "never animate opacity alone" rule holds.
4. Sweep simplified to a literal `rotate(azimuth)` on `[data-dish-spin]` about
   `DISH_CENTRE`, per the explicit spec ("rotate() on the whole icon group").
   The [006] `scaleX = cos θ` foreshortening is gone. `SWEEP_DURATION` (26s) and
   the 5 × 72° `SWEEP_ORDER` card-highlight sync are unchanged.
5. Three nested groups keep transform ownership separate:
   `[data-dish-body]` scroll pulse → `[data-dish-spin]` idle rotation →
   `[data-dish-art]` scrubbed reveal.

**Verified**
`npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · `GET / 200`, dev server `✓ Compiled` · 0 remaining
`DISH_PATHS` / `[data-dish-draw]` references · DOM confirms the svg, all three
groups, `fill="url(#wcDishGrad)"` resolving, and 400 px rendered width · inlined
path asserted byte-identical to the vendored source file.

**Known visual consequence — flagged, not a bug**
The asset has a pedestal base, i.e. a ground reference. A full 360° `rotate()`
therefore carries it upside-down through the second half of every revolution
(clearest at 216°). This is what the spec asked for and is implemented as asked.
If it reads wrong, the one-line fix is in the sweep tween: swap
`deg: 360` for a yoyo over a limited arc, e.g.
`gsap.to(azimuth, { deg: 50, duration: 13, yoyo: true, repeat: -1, ease: 'sine.inOut' })`
— the 72° segment mapping keeps working, it just covers a narrower arc.

**Not verified** — the preview pane still renders 0 rAF frames, so the rotation
and the highlight transitions do not advance there. Geometry, licence, build and
DOM wiring are confirmed; the motion needs a real browser.

**Revert**
Restore `components/WhyChoose.tsx` to [006] (the `DISH_PATHS` primitives, the
0 0 400 430 viewBox, `[data-dish-draw]` stroke reveal, and the `scaleX = cos θ`
sweep) and delete `public/vendor/`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[008] — WhyChoose: dish becomes a looping video; highlight synced to its clock
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/WhyChoose.tsx`, new `public/media/`, removed `public/vendor/`.

**Asset**
`public/media/satellite-dish-orbit.mp4` — user-supplied 3D render of a dish
completing one full revolution. ffprobe: **10.005s, 240 frames @ 24fps,
1280×720, h264, 2.5 MB**. One revolution per loop, so a fifth of the loop is
exactly 72° of rotation. A poster still (`-poster.jpg`, 11 KB) was extracted from
frame 0 with ffmpeg so nothing pops in before the video decodes.

**Change**
1. Every trace of the static dish is gone: `DISH_ART`, `DISH_CENTRE`, the
   `0 0 15 15` `<svg>`, `[data-dish-art]`, `[data-dish-spin]`, the `wcDishGrad`
   gradient, and `SWEEP_DURATION`. `public/vendor/` (the [007] CC0 SVG and its
   licence record) deleted — no longer referenced. Verified 0 orphan refs.
2. `<video autoplay loop muted playsinline>` in its place, inside
   `[data-dish-body]` at the same `max-w-[400px]`. Cropped `aspect-[4/3]` +
   `object-cover`: the footage is 16:9 but the dish sits centred, so the crop
   fills the column's height without cutting the subject. A radial mask
   (`black 55% → transparent 82%`) feathers the video's own light-blue backdrop
   into the section rather than leaving a hard rectangle — the render's palette
   already matches brand blue, so it blends rather than sits on top.
3. **Highlight retargeted from the ping cycle to the loop.** The old driver
   tweened an `azimuth` proxy and derived the segment from its angle. Now the
   segment is read from **`video.currentTime`**, not from an independent timer.
   This matters: a free-running timer drifts out of phase the instant the loop
   stalls, buffers, or the tab throttles, and the highlight would slowly slide
   away from the rotation it is meant to describe. Reading the video's own clock
   re-syncs on every tick. Falls back to wall-clock only if `duration` is not yet
   finite (still decoding, or autoplay blocked).
4. Driven by `setInterval(…, 120)`, deliberately not the GSAP ticker — the
   ticker is rAF-driven and stops dead in a background tab, which would freeze
   the highlight mid-card. Cleared in the `useGSAP` cleanup alongside `mm.revert()`.
5. Scroll reveal simplified to a `scale 0.78 → 1` on `[data-dish-body]`. The old
   reveal also rotated; that would have fought the rotation the footage is
   already doing. `[data-dish-ring]` is now an HTML span, so its tween moved from
   `strokeOpacity` to `opacity`.
6. `preload="metadata"`, not `auto` — below `lg` this column is `display:none`,
   and preloading 2.5 MB a mobile visitor never sees is the wrong trade for a
   rural-internet audience.

**Verified — including runtime, for the first time this section**
Because the highlight runs on `setInterval` rather than rAF, it actually advances
in the preview pane, so this was observed rather than inferred:
- `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · `GET / 200` · **no server errors**.
- `/media/satellite-dish-orbit.mp4` → HTTP 200, `video/mp4`, 2,615,444 bytes;
  poster → HTTP 200, `image/jpeg`.
- Video element: `readyState 4`, `paused: false`, `duration 10.005` (matches
  ffprobe exactly), `1280×720`, `error: null`, autoplay/loop/muted/playsInline
  all true, poster set.
- **Highlight cycle observed live over ~11.5s**, transitions at
  `0.07s→01`, `2.07s→02`, `4.07s→04`, `6.33s→05`, `8.18s→03`, then wrapping
  cleanly back to `01`. Segment boundaries are 0 / 2.001 / 4.002 / 6.003 / 8.004,
  so every switch landed within one 120 ms tick. Exactly one card lit at any
  moment. Order matches `SWEEP_ORDER` ['01','02','04','05','03'].
- 0 remaining `DISH_ART` / `DISH_CENTRE` / `data-dish-art` / `data-dish-spin` /
  `data-dish-draw` / `wcDishGrad` / `SWEEP_DURATION` references.

**Not verified** — the video's *visual* fit at desktop width. The pane viewport is
below `lg`, where this column is `display:none` (measured 0×0), so the 4:3 crop
and the radial mask feather have not been seen at 400 px. Frames were inspected
directly via ffmpeg and the palette matches; the composition at final size needs
a real browser.

**Revert**
Restore `components/WhyChoose.tsx` to [007] (the `DISH_ART` CC0 path, the
`0 0 15 15` svg, `[data-dish-spin]` rotate sweep and the azimuth-proxy driver),
restore `public/vendor/` from the Commons URL in [007], and delete
`public/media/`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[009] — WhyChoose: highlight order, timeupdate driver, 350ms crossfade
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/WhyChoose.tsx`, `app/globals.css`.
A tightening of [008]. The segment mechanism was already 5 equal fifths read
from `video.currentTime`; this fixes order, driver and timing.

**Change**
1. **`SWEEP_ORDER` changed from clockwise-spatial to numeric.** Was
   `['01','02','04','05','03']` — the order the cards sit around the stage
   going clockwise. Now `['01','02','03','04','05']`, i.e. card N in segment N
   as specified. Worth knowing: 03 and 04 sit lower-left and lower-right, so the
   highlight now *zigzags* across the layout instead of travelling around it.
   Reading order was specified over spatial order, so that is what shipped.
2. **`timeupdate` is now the primary driver**, per spec. The 120ms interval is
   kept as a backstop — `timeupdate` fires only ~4×/sec and stops entirely while
   paused, stalled or throttled, which would leave a segment boundary up to
   250ms late. Both handlers read the same `video.currentTime`, so the interval
   cannot introduce drift; it only samples more often.
3. Added `play`, `seeked` and `visibilitychange` listeners so a clock jump
   re-syncs immediately rather than on the next tick. All four listeners plus
   the interval are torn down in the `useGSAP` cleanup.
4. **Crossfade retimed 700ms → 350ms** and centralised. Timing now lives in one
   block in `globals.css` covering chip, chipglow, connector, flow line and h3;
   the competing `duration-700` Tailwind utilities were removed from the
   component so there is a single source of truth. Outgoing and incoming cards
   share the same duration and `ease-in-out` curve, so the handover reads as one
   glow moving rather than two independent on/off switches.

**Verified (runtime, observed not inferred)**
- `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · no server errors.
- Computed transitions on a live pair: chip `0.35s / color, border-color,
  box-shadow` · chipglow `0.35s / filter, transform, opacity` · connector
  `0.35s / stroke-width, stroke-opacity` · h3 `0.35s / color`.
- **Full loop observed over 12.5s** after a hard reload: `0.06s→01`, `2.06s→02`,
  `4.26s→03`, `6.06s→04`, `8.06s→05`, then wrapping to `01`. Boundaries are
  0 / 2.001 / 4.002 / 6.003 / 8.004 — every switch inside one sampling window.
- `maxLitAtOnce: 1` and `everZero: false` across every sample: exactly one card
  lit at all times, never a gap with none lit.
- **Seek re-sync proved directly**: with the video *paused*, seeking to
  7.0 / 9.0 / 0.5 / 3.0 / 5.0s produced 04 / 05 / 01 / 02 / 03 — all five
  correct. This is the behaviour that a separate independent timer could not
  give, since a paused video advances no clock.

**Caught during this pass:** the first post-edit run still showed the old
`01→02→04→05→03` order. Fast Refresh had swapped the CSS but not re-run the
`useGSAP` effect holding the old array in its closure. A hard reload was needed
to see the change — worth remembering when verifying edits to constants captured
inside that effect.

**Not verified** — the intermediate frames of the 350ms crossfade. The pane
renders 0 rAF frames, so CSS transitions never advance in it; the declared
durations are confirmed but the fade itself has not been watched.

**Revert**
In `components/WhyChoose.tsx` restore `SWEEP_ORDER` to
`['01','02','04','05','03']`, drop the `timeupdate` / `play` / `seeked` /
`visibilitychange` listeners and their teardown; in `app/globals.css` set the
`[data-wc-pair]` transition block back to 700ms.

