--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[018] — Connected-life stage: container aspect matched to the clip
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/features/DeviceStage.tsx`.

**Diagnosis first: the clip was never cropped.** Measured before changing
anything — `object-fit: contain`, `object-position: 50% 50%`, and **no ancestor
transform anywhere in the chain** (walked video → body checking `transform`,
`scale`, `zoom`). The real state was the opposite of cropping: a 16:9 clip in a
4:3 box rendered **620×349 inside 620×465**, so the whole frame was visible but
undersized, with 58px of dead space above and below. Extracting a frame from the
installed file confirmed the router and Wi-Fi lines sit well inside the source
composition, so the tightness was not in the asset either.

**Fix** — the second option offered: container aspect ratio matched to the clip's
own. `aspect-[4/3]` → `aspect-video`, and `max-w-[620px]` → `max-w-[720px]` so
the plate keeps the visual mass the taller box had given it. With the ratios
equal, `cover` and `contain` resolve identically; `object-cover object-center` is
used since full-bleed fill was the stated preference.

**Verified** — `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · `object-fit: cover`,
`object-position: 50% 50%` · native AR 1.7778 vs container AR 1.7778,
`ratiosMatch: true` · box 720×405, rendered frame 720×405, **cropped 0px on both
axes** · 0 ancestor transforms · `error: null`, duration 9.125.

Section height at this column drops 465 → 405; nothing else in the section
changed.

**Revert** — `aspect-video max-w-[720px]` → `aspect-[4/3] max-w-[620px]` and
`object-cover object-center` → `object-contain`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[019] — Connected-life stage: 1.12x crop-in
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/features/DeviceStage.tsx`.

Requested tighter crop. `transform: scale(1.12)` on the video element, clipped by
the container's existing `overflow-hidden`, so the frame's edges run past the
visible box the way a cropped hero video does. [018] had matched the container to
the clip's 16:9 with zero crop; this deliberately reintroduces a small one to cut
the navy padding around the devices.

**Kept on the video, not the container** — GSAP's entrance in `Features.tsx`
animates `[data-ds-stage]`'s own scale (`from { scale: 0.94 }`). Putting the
overscale on the stage would mean two systems writing one element's transform,
and the entrance would snap the crop back to 1.

**Verified** — `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · computed transform
`matrix(1.12, 0, 0, 1.12, 0, 0)`, scaleX and scaleY both 1.12 ·
`object-fit: cover`, `object-position: 50% 50%` · stage 720x405 with the video
painting 806x454 → **86px horizontal / 49px vertical overhang**, `overflow:
hidden` confirmed on the stage, `clipped: true` · `error: null`.

Before/after was rendered for the user as a static comparison at the real 16:9
box (frame extracted with ffmpeg, embedded as a data URI, shown at both scales) —
the preview pane cannot screenshot, so this stands in for one.

**Revert** — drop the `style={{ transform: 'scale(1.12)' }}` from the video.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[020] — Connected-life stage: new 4:3 clip
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/features/DeviceStage.tsx`, `public/media/connected-devices.*`.

**Asset** — "Video Project 21 (1).mp4", 1440x1080 / 10.0s / 30fps / **13.6 MB**.
Same composition as the previous clip but framed 4:3 with the devices filling the
frame rather than floating in navy.

Processed before shipping:
· Loop seam was bad — first vs last frame **18.75 dB** PSNR. Tail-over-head
  crossfade lifts it to **32.40 dB**.
· Downscaled 1440x1080 → 1200x900 and re-encoded at crf 24. **13.6 MB → 1.16 MB.**
  At a 620px display box, 1200px wide is still 2x for retina; shipping 13.6 MB to
  a rural-internet audience was not defensible.
· Duration 10.0 → 9.1s (the crossfade consumes the overlap).

**Container re-matched** — this source is **4:3**, not the previous 16:9, so the
box follows it: `aspect-video max-w-[720px]` → `aspect-[4/3] max-w-[620px]`,
which also returns the section to the original illustration's 620x465 footprint.

**Overscale removed.** [019]'s `scale(1.12)` was added to crop wide navy padding
out of the *previous* clip. This one is already framed tight — the corner Wi-Fi
glyphs sit within ~4% of the edges — so the same overscale would clip content
instead of background. Dropped, and noted in the file so the reasoning survives.

**Verified** — `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · native 1200x900 (AR 1.3333) vs box
620x465 (AR 1.3333), `ratiosMatch: true`, **cropped 0px on both axes** ·
`transform: none`, `object-fit: cover`, `object-position: 50% 50%` · duration 9.1,
`error: null`, loop/muted/autoplay set · asset serves 200 · tab list (4 rows) and
"Whole Home Wi-Fi" heading unchanged.

**Caught during verification:** the browser served the *cached* previous file
(reported 1280x720 / 9.125s) after the swap, since the path is unchanged. Had to
re-request with a query string to confirm the new asset. Worth remembering — a
same-path media swap will look like it did nothing until a hard reload.

**Revert** — restore the previous `connected-devices.*` pair, set the container
back to `aspect-video max-w-[720px]` and re-add `style={{ transform: 'scale(1.12)' }}`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[021] — Hero: the actual cause of the "fading" and the right-edge seam
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/Hero.tsx`, `app/globals.css`.

Third report of these two. [013] and [015] fixed real things but not the ones
being seen. Probing the live page instead of re-asserting found both.

**"Content fading" — the CTA block was stuck at opacity 0.**
Sampling computed opacity at y = 0/150/300/450/600 showed
`form: 0` at **every** position, while the copy wrapper read `transform:none` /
opacity 1. That is why the earlier checks passed: they measured the parallax
wrapper, not the entrance tweens inside it. The hero timeline is `.pause()`d
while `document.hidden`, and every entrance was a `.from({ opacity: 0 })` — so
the block only ever becomes visible if the timeline runs. Anything that stops it
(background-tab load, throttled ticker, GSAP failing) leaves the CTA invisible,
and a partially-run timeline reads exactly like a scroll-linked fade.

**Removed `opacity: 0` from all 7 hero entrance tweens** (eyebrow, headline,
sub, offer, form, trust, HUD). They now move only. Content is legible from first
paint regardless of what happens to GSAP. This is the same rule already written
into globals for the headline characters; it simply had not been applied to the
rest of the block.

**Right-edge seam — the scrollbar thumb.**
Verified the geometry is not at fault: video right edge = section right edge =
1270 = `documentElement.clientWidth`, nav fully transparent at top, hero top 0,
no absolutely-positioned child leaving a gap. The only thing occupying the right
edge is the 10px scrollbar, whose **thumb was still solid `#cbd5e1`** — [013]
made the *track* transparent but left the thumb a bright grey bar running down
the dark hero. Now `rgba(148,163,184,0.45)` (hover 0.7), translucent so it reads
acceptably over both the dark hero and the light sections.

**Verified after reload** — opacity sampled at y = 0/250/500/750:
eyebrow, h1, sub, offer, form and trust all **1.000 at every position**,
`allFullyVisibleAlways: true`. `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0.

**Revert** — re-add `opacity: 0` to the entrance tweens in `Hero.tsx` and set
`::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb` back to `#cbd5e1` / `#94a3b8`.

