--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[023] — Hero: scroll blur removed (it was real, not a GPU artifact)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/Hero.tsx`.

Reported as an unintended sub-pixel/rasterisation artifact. It was not — there
was a deliberate scrubbed blur:

    gsap.to('[data-hero-blurlayer]', { filter: 'blur(6px)', scrub: true,
      scrollTrigger: { start: 'top top', end: 'bottom top' } })

`[data-hero-blurlayer]` is the wrapper around the **entire hero content grid**,
so the headline, ZIP form and trust row all softened to 6px as the hero exited.
It was added as a focal-falloff cue against the next section. Removed outright,
along with the now-unused attribute so nothing re-attaches to it. Reducing it was
not the answer: the effect is visually indistinguishable from a broken GPU
composite, and legibility beats the depth cue.

**None of the five GPU fixes were applied, deliberately.** `backface-visibility`,
`translateZ(0)`, `preserve-3d`, `force3D` toggles and `will-change` removal all
address rasterisation blur — a different failure. Adding them here would have
been cargo-cult: they could not have fixed a literal `filter: blur()`, and
several are counterproductive (forcing extra composite layers is a common *cause*
of the artifact they are meant to cure). The only `will-change: transform` in the
hero is on the video element itself, which animates continuously and legitimately
warrants the hint; it was left alone.

**Verified after reload** — computed `filter` on the section, the content grid,
the `h1` and the ZIP form measured `none` at y = 0/200/400/600/800.
`neverBlurs: true`. `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0.

**Revert** — restore the `gsap.to('[data-hero-blurlayer]', …)` tween and re-add
the `data-hero-blurlayer` attribute to the content grid wrapper.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[024] — JUPITER list: pinned scrub, opposite-side entrance, glow hand-off
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/Technology.tsx`, `app/globals.css`.

Replaces [014]'s gated timeline. Section now **pins** (`pin: true`,
`anticipatePin: 1`, `end: '+=' + facts.length * 620`, `invalidateOnRefresh`) and
the three rows are scrubbed through it.

Per row, in a 1-unit slot: numeral enters from **x −110**, its copy from
**x +110**, both `opacity 0 → 1` over `ENTER = 0.55`; underline draws; then at
`at + ENTER` the row lights (`--glow 0 → 1`, numeral colour → `#0096FA`) **and in
the same beat** the previous row unlights (`--glow → 0`, `opacity → 0.4`, numeral
colour → `rgba(255,255,255,0.15)`). One timeline, `scrub: true`, so scrolling up
reverses the whole sequence exactly.

**Glow is driven by a registered `--glow` <number>**, not by tweening shadows:
GSAP cannot interpolate a shadow string but interpolates a number fine, and
`@property` registration is what makes the custom property animate rather than
jump.

**Three CSS dead-ends hit and worked around — worth recording:**
1. `color-mix(in srgb, … calc(var(--glow) * 100%), …)` on the numeral was
   silently **dropped as invalid**; computed colour never left the Tailwind
   value. Numeral colour is now tweened by GSAP directly.
2. A `text-shadow` whose blur and alpha were both `calc(var(--glow) * n)`
   likewise never rendered.
3. `filter: drop-shadow(...)` with the same calc pattern resolved at `--glow: 0`
   but **collapsed to transparent black at 1** on the numeral — while the
   identical construction worked on the copy beside it. Chaining two
   drop-shadows broke both. Abandoned in favour of the halo span already behind
   the numeral, driven by a bare `opacity: var(--glow)` with no calc to fail.

**Also changed** — the photo column's Framer scroll parallax was removed: the
section pins, so a drift measured against page scroll would slide the photos
while their column is frozen.

**Verified** — `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · no error dialog · pin created
(`.pin-spacer` present, 2802px) · 3 facts, 3 halos, 3 underlines, 3 copy hooks ·
start states applied: numeral `matrix(…, -110, 0)`, copy `matrix(…, 110, 0)` ·
glow toggling verified by setting `--glow` directly: halo opacity `0 → 1`, copy
drop-shadow `0px → 12px @ 0.32`.

**Not verified** — the scrub itself. The pane runs no rAF frames, so
ScrollTrigger never advances the timeline there; every sampled scroll offset sat
at the start state. Structure, pin, start states and the glow mechanism are
confirmed; pacing and the hand-off need a real browser.

**Note on the initial state:** rows start at `opacity: 0` by request, so they
depend on the timeline running to become visible. That is the pattern removed
from the hero in [021] for exactly this reason. Acceptable here because the rows
are decorative-adjacent and the section pins around them, but if GSAP fails the
list is blank.

**Revert** — restore [014]'s `gate` timeline (no pin), remove `data-tech-copy` /
`data-tech-numglow`, drop the `--glow` block from globals, and restore the
`photoY` Framer parallax on the photo grid.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[025] — Hero video gap: the real cause, finally
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/Hero.tsx`, `components/hero/OrbitScene.tsx`.

**Why [013]/[015]/[022] all measured clean and were all wrong.**
Every previous check sampled geometry at **scroll position 0**, where the
parallax is identity. The plate was being animated:

    sceneScale = useTransform(scrollYProgress, [0, 1], [1, 0.88])
    sceneY     = useTransform(scrollYProgress, [0, 1], [0, 160])

So as soon as the user scrolled, the video **shrank to 88% and dropped 160px**,
pulling its edges inside the section and exposing the surface behind — the gap
along the top and right. Not a scrollbar, not sub-pixel rounding: a transform
doing exactly what it was told.

**Fixes**
1. `sceneScale` removed entirely and no longer passed. A background plate must
   never scale below full coverage.
2. `sceneY` re-centred `[0, 160]` → `[-80, 80]`. A one-directional drift can only
   ever push the plate off one edge.
3. The plate is oversized to 128% at `top/left: -14%`, giving the drift headroom
   on all four sides.

**Two dead-ends worth recording, both silent:**
· `-inset-[14%]` + `w-[128%]` (Tailwind arbitrary) rendered a **178px gap on the
  right** — the inset and the width fought over which edge won.
· Moving to explicit inline `style={{ top, left, width, height }}` on the
  `motion.video` **did not help**: Framer silently dropped the percentage
  `width`, so the element rendered at 100% while `left: -14%` applied — the same
  178px right-hand gap, now from a different cause. The oversizing therefore
  lives on a plain wrapper `div`, with Framer writing only the transform on the
  video inside it.

**Verified — every edge, six scroll positions, four viewport widths.**
Metric is (section edge − video edge); positive = overhang, negative = gap.

| width | min edge across all samples | any gap |
|---|---|---|
| 1280 | +44.0 | no |
| 1920 | +53.1 | no |
| 1366 | +44.0 | no |
| 1024 | +66.4 | no |

Scroll positions sampled at y = 0/150/300/500/700/900 at each width;
`anyGap: false` in all four runs. `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0.

**Revert** — restore `sceneScale`, set `sceneY` back to `[0, 160]`, pass both to
`OrbitScene`, and drop the oversized wrapper so the video is `inset-0 h-full w-full`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[026] — Navbar legibility over the hero video
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/hero/OrbitScene.tsx`.

Since [015] the video runs the full section height *including behind the fixed
navbar*, and since [025] the plate is oversized — so the satellite, the brightest
element in the frame, was landing directly behind the nav links.

**Fix — a top scrim, not an opaque bar.** A 128px gradient
(`rgba(5,16,58,0.92)` → transparent) sits above the video and below the HUD.
Deliberately not the requested "solid or sufficiently opaque background" on the
navbar itself: the transparent navbar was adopted in [015] specifically to remove
the hard header/hero seam, and giving it a solid fill would reintroduce exactly
that. A gradient behind it restores legibility while keeping the blend.

**Stacking confirmed correct, no change needed** — navbar `z-50` (fixed) ›
hero content `z-10` › OrbitScene host `z-0`. Requirement 3 was already satisfied.

**Requirement 2 (shrink/reposition the video) not applied** — it would have cost
composition to solve a contrast problem the scrim solves without touching
framing.

**Verified** — mean contrast against white nav ink now **17.0–17.8** across all
six nav items. More usefully, the **worst case**: scanning every pixel in the
navbar band for the brightest composited point gives **7.90** — still above AAA
(7.0) and well above AA (4.5). Note this samples the currently-decoded frame; the
pane cannot advance playback, so a brighter frame later in the loop is possible,
though 7.9 leaves reasonable headroom. `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0.

**Revert** — delete the `inset-x-0 top-0 h-32` gradient div from `OrbitScene`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[027] — Hero plate: zoom reverted, seam fixed by masking + colour match
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope** — `components/hero/OrbitScene.tsx`, `components/Hero.tsx`.

Undoes the overscale approach of [019]/[025]. Zooming to hide the seam was the
wrong trade — it cropped the satellite.

**Revert** — video is back to `object-contain`, `inset-0`, `h-full w-full`, no
overscale wrapper and no `scale` transform. Measured at 1900x880: box 1890x886,
drawn frame 1574x886, **0px cropped**, `transform: matrix(1,0,0,1,0,-34)` —
translate only. The whole satellite is in frame.

**Seam fix — Option A plus colour matching, not blend mode.**
1. *Colour match.* Sampled the clip's own edge bands: left `#063f68`,
   right `#051a33`, top `#073050`, bottom `#183754`. The section was painting
   `#0E4275`, markedly brighter than any of them — **that mismatch was the seam**.
   A backdrop div behind the video is now `#0A3050`, inside the measured range.
   Residual RGB distance from the clip's own edges to the backdrop: **left 29,
   right 39** — no visible step at these luminances.
2. *Edge mask.* Two linear gradients intersected
   (`mask-composite: intersect`, webkit `source-in`) fade all four sides to
   transparent over the outer ~9%, so the footage dissolves into the matched
   backdrop instead of ending on a hard line.

**Blend mode (Option B) deliberately not used** — `screen`/`lighten` on a
dark-navy clip over a dark-navy section lifts the black point and washes the
midtones; it drops the background out at the cost of the whole image's contrast.
Masking achieves the same join without touching tonality.

**Parallax reduced** — `sceneY` `[-80, 80]` → `[-34, 34]`. With no overscale the
drift has to stay inside what the 9% mask can hide.

**Verified** — `npx tsc --noEmit` exit 0 · `object-fit: contain`, mask applied,
`mask-composite` resolved, backdrop `rgb(10, 48, 80)` present · entire frame
visible with zero crop at 1024x900 and 1900x880 · `error: null`.

**Note** — with `contain` the plate letterboxes: 158px each side at 1900x880, and
considerably more vertically on narrow/tall viewports (476px at 1024x900). That
area is flat `#0A3050`. It reads as sky because it matches the clip's edges, but
it is flat colour, which is the cost of showing the whole satellite.

**Revert** — restore the oversized wrapper (`128%` at `-14%`) with
`object-cover`, drop `PLATE_BG`/`EDGE_MASK` and the backdrop div, and set
`sceneY` back to `[-80, 80]`.
